   "text": {
    "L1": "A thermometer measures temperature. The right thermometer depends on what we are measuring.",
    "L2": "Here is a myth: a clinical thermometer can measure boiling water. That is not true.",
    "L3": "A clinical thermometer measures body temperature. Its range is about 35 °C to 42 °C, because body temperature stays in this range and varies a little.",
    "L4": "A laboratory thermometer has a wider range, usually −10 °C to 110 °C. It can measure other things, such as water.",
    "L5": "To read a scale, find one small division and count. Ten divisions from 20 °C to 30 °C make each division 1 °C.",
    "L6": "Keep a lab thermometer upright, with its bulb in the liquid and away from the bottom and sides.",
    "L7": "Read it while it is still in the liquid, with your eye level with the top of the column. Then record the value with its unit.",
    "L8": "Digital thermometers are safer than mercury ones. Now arrange the reading steps in order."
   },
